# refund-api local environment (direnv).
#
# STILL no 1Password-sourced secrets as of T19: .env.example now documents
# REFUND_S3_* and NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN (the vars T9/T13 will consume), but
# neither has a real 1Password vault item yet —
#   - NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN follows auth's own local-dev posture (see
#     auth/.env.example, notify-api/.env.example): it's a shared secret
#     across THREE services once T13 lands, but for local dev it's just a
#     manually-generated value (`openssl rand -hex 32`) pasted into each
#     service's plain `.env` — auth/.envrc doesn't source it via `op` either.
#     Only the PRODUCTION Railway value is 1Password-sourced (infra/README.md
#     § Variable reference: `AIScream / OperAI - NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN`).
#   - REFUND_S3_* has NO 1Password item yet at all (no bucket has been
#     provisioned — see .env.example's own note and this task's final
#     report). Do NOT invent a placeholder vault path here.
#
# Once a real EU-region bucket + credentials exist in 1Password (human action,
# pre-T9), extend this file with the same pattern auth/.envrc uses: guard on a
# gitignored `.env.cached`, populate it with one `op read` per secret against
# the real vault item, then `source .env.cached`.
#
# Until then, direnv simply loads the local, gitignored .env (see .env.example
# for the template — copy it to .env and fill in values, same as every other
# Operai backend).
dotenv_if_exists .env
